from pathlib import Path
import json
s=json.loads(Path('summary.json').read_text());labels={'participation':'Effective load-bearing fraction','maxShare':'Largest fiber force share','spread':'Damage radius / diagonal','patch':'Largest damage patch fraction'}
lo=min(0,min(x['ci'][0]*100 for x in s));hi=max(0,max(x['ci'][1]*100 for x in s));lo-=1;hi+=1
scale=lambda x:480+(x-lo)/(hi-lo)*510
svg='<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 1200 680" role="img" aria-label="Clustered minus distributed differences with seed-bootstrap intervals"><rect width="1200" height="680" fill="#faf8f0"/><g font-family="Arial" fill="#203b32"><text x="25" y="40" font-size="26">Force concentration and damage extent across seeds and angles</text><text x="25" y="75" font-size="18">Paired differences ×100 · exploratory 95% intervals across seeds · 24 added links</text>'
svg+=f'<path d="M {scale(0)} 100 V 580" stroke="#888" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>'
for i,r in enumerate(s):
 y=125+i*58;c='#3c775b' if r['family']=='isotropic' else '#805694';a,b=[v*100 for v in r['ci']];m=r['meanDelta']*100
 svg+=f'<text x="25" y="{y}" font-size="17">{r["family"]} · {labels[r["metric"]]}</text><path d="M {scale(a)} {y-5} H {scale(b)}" stroke="{c}" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="{scale(m)}" cy="{y-5}" r="5" fill="{c}"/><text x="1010" y="{y}" font-size="16">{m:+.2f} [{a:+.2f}, {b:+.2f}]</text>'
svg+='<text x="25" y="625" font-size="18">Force metrics: 120/120 pairs per architecture. Damage metrics: 73–86/120; see coverage table.</text><text x="25" y="655" font-size="17">Lower load-bearing fraction = more concentration. Smaller radius = less spatially extensive damage.</text></g></svg>'
Path('comparison.svg').write_text(svg)
html='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Force concentration and damage robustness</title><style>body{font:17px system-ui;color:#203b32;background:#faf8f0;max-width:1200px;margin:30px auto;padding:20px}p{line-height:1.6}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:15px}th,td{padding:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #ccd4c7;text-align:left}img{width:100%}.table{overflow:auto}a{color:#286550}</style><h1>Does crosslink placement change force concentration and damage spreading?</h1><p>10 seeds × 12 loading angles × 2 architectures × 2 placement patterns = 480 configurations, each solved at 480 and 960 load steps. Added count 24; unchanged dimensionless mechanics.</p><img src="comparison.svg" alt="Paired contrasts and uncertainty intervals across seeds"><div class="table">'''+Path('table.html').read_text()+'''</div><p>Differences are clustered minus distributed. For fraction metrics, ×100 gives percentage-point differences; for damage radius it gives percentage points of the unit-square diagonal. Positive/negative counts include all resolved angle pairs; the mean/interval use seeds with at least six resolved angles. Ties account for remaining pairs.</p><p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> Clustered links concentrate early fiber forces in both architectures. They also tend to localize damage spatially at a matched damage level. The modular largest-fiber share and damage-patch contrasts remain uncertain. Neither force concentration nor spatial damage extent alone determines work capacity.</p><p><strong>Limits:</strong> Damage estimates exclude resolution-sensitive and poorly matched damage stages; complete-case selection may bias them. Intervals are exploratory, conditional on resolved cases, and unadjusted for four correlated metrics. Angles within each seed are grouped. These results cover one connector count and a small network model; they are not calibrated tissue-aging predictions.</p><p><a href="METHODS.md">Definitions and analysis methods</a> · <a href="results.csv">All contrasts: CSV</a> · <a href="checked-results.json">Fine/coarse results and flags</a> · <a href="summary.json">Summary data</a> · <a href="comparison.svg">Download vector plot</a> · <a href="../force-replay/">Watch the four-case replay</a></p></html>'''
Path('index.html').write_text(html)
